From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 17:22:52 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ether: the 7.1 legacy IMS AIDL surface, generated and
 verified on the wire

ims.apk implements com.android.ims.internal.IImsService, the pre-P IMS binding Android 9 deleted.
frameworks/opt/telephony still carries the compat path (ImsServiceControllerCompat,
MmTelFeatureCompatAdapter) behind android.telephony.ims.compat.ImsService, so the gap is one
translation layer, not a rewrite. This is the interface surface that layer talks through; the bridge
itself is the next patch.

The interfaces live in org.codeaurora.ims.legacy, matching the rename already applied to the rebuilt
apk. Method ORDER is load-bearing: AIDL numbers transactions by declaration order, and the far end is
stock's compiled 2016 code that cannot be recompiled to agree with us. Hand-transcribing 148
transactions is exactly where a silent, hardware-only failure comes from, so gen-legacy-aidl.py reads
method order from each $Stub's TRANSACTION_ constants and signatures from the interface's descriptors.

All fourteen interfaces: IImsCallSession (28 transactions), IImsCallSessionListener (30), IImsUt (18),
IImsVideoCallProvider (11), IImsRegistrationListener (11), IImsConfig (9), IImsUtListener (7),
IImsVideoCallCallback (7), ImsConfigListener (4), IImsService (16), and four two-method ones.

verify-legacy-aidl.sh checks the BUILT apk -- not the .aidl source -- against the stock binary: all 14
match on every transaction code and descriptor, and IImsService numbers open=1 through
getMultiEndpointInterface=16 as 7.1 does. The verifier is negative-tested; corrupting one code fails
it.

Seven parcelables come with it. Their wire order is not their declaration order --
ImsCallForwardInfo writes condition, status, toA, number, timeSeconds, serviceClass -- and
ImsConferenceState marshals a map as size then String/Bundle pairs.

aidl/android/view/Surface.aidl declares a framework parcelable that has no .aidl of its own; the
platform resolves it through framework.aidl, which a device module does not get.
